<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Emesene dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">64 BCE – 72 CE (approx.) · capital Emesa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Emesa, an Arab priestly kingdom in central Syria whose descendants included the Roman empress Julia Domna and emperor Elagabalus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sampsigeramus-i">Sampsigeramus I</a>, an Arab chief, controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes</a> valley around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, modern Homs, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> collapsed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> recognised him as a client ruler in 64 BCE, founding a dynasty that would last over a century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers were priests of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabal">Elagabal</a>, worshipped as a black stone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>. They served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and soldiers and grew wealthy on the caravan trade of the Syrian desert. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iamblichus-i-of-emesa">Iamblichus I</a> was executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, but the dynasty soon regained power under Augustus.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-julius-sohaemus">Gaius Julius Sohaemus</a>, the kingdom was absorbed into Roman <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> around 72 CE. The priestly family survived, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julia-domna">Julia Domna</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>; her great-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> became emperor in 218 CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emesene-dynasty.jpg" alt="Emesene dynasty" width="320" height="387"><figcaption>Image: Julia Domna Ba'al · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KingdomOfEmesa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>64 BCE – 72 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Emesa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sampsigeramus I, Iamblichus I, Sohaemus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cult of Elagabal</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Emesa, Emesa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=4&amp;focus=emesene-dynasty" aria-label="Emesene dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5618 910 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emesene dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5938 1166L5304 942M5938 1166L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5938 1166h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5938 1166h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=4&amp;focus=emesene-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
